WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.680
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone. Thank
you so much for coming.

00:00:07.681 --> 00:00:08.880
We're very happy to have with

00:00:08.881 --> 00:00:10.935
us Professor Miroslaw Staron,

00:00:10.936 --> 00:00:12.990
from the University
of Gothenburg and

00:00:12.991 --> 00:00:15.390
Chalmers in Gothenburg Sweden.

00:00:15.391 --> 00:00:18.150
He's going to talk to us
today about his work on

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:21.020
autonomous AI-based measurement
systems in Ericsson,

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.670
Volvo, and a bunch
of other companies.

00:00:23.671 --> 00:00:26.010
So, without much further ado.

00:00:26.011 --> 00:00:29.610
&gt;&gt; All right, great.
Thanks. First of all,

00:00:29.611 --> 00:00:31.710
thanks for having me.

00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:35.685
I really appreciate coming
over to Microsoft Research.

00:00:35.686 --> 00:00:38.480
So, who am I?

00:00:38.481 --> 00:00:40.250
I'm as Seth said,

00:00:40.251 --> 00:00:42.050
Professor in software
engineering.

00:00:42.051 --> 00:00:43.370
I work mostly with

00:00:43.371 --> 00:00:45.620
software measurement
in different areas.

00:00:45.621 --> 00:00:47.345
I'm mostly working
with companies like

00:00:47.346 --> 00:00:49.420
Ericsson and Volvo on that.

00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:52.100
I'm also active in
standardization committees,

00:00:52.101 --> 00:00:53.480
most recently in the

00:00:53.481 --> 00:00:57.685
25,000 standards and
revision of those.

00:00:57.686 --> 00:00:59.840
Those are basically the pictures

00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:01.835
of things that we're
doing together with,

00:01:01.836 --> 00:01:03.695
in this case, Ericsson and Volvo.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:05.510
So, different kinds
of dashboards and

00:01:05.511 --> 00:01:08.620
the measurement system
for decision support.

00:01:08.621 --> 00:01:10.520
I brought the book that we

00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:12.515
wrote together with
Wilhelm, my colleague.

00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:16.860
You can browse it and I will
leave the copy with Nacci.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.945
Yeah, so this is
basically our team.

00:01:21.946 --> 00:01:26.780
It's both from
academia and industry,

00:01:26.781 --> 00:01:29.070
and both me and

00:01:29.071 --> 00:01:30.570
Wilhelm have been
working together for the

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:34.470
past 12,13 years now on
measurement systems.

00:01:34.471 --> 00:01:38.100
We actually both,

00:01:38.101 --> 00:01:39.720
we're located about
200 meters away

00:01:39.721 --> 00:01:41.370
from each other to
different places.

00:01:41.371 --> 00:01:45.645
So, we work a lot on
software measurement.

00:01:45.646 --> 00:01:47.820
The context of
the whole collaboration

00:01:47.821 --> 00:01:49.645
is the Software Center,

00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:53.360
which is 11 companies
and five universities.

00:01:53.361 --> 00:01:56.870
It's a collaboration
that is quite similar

00:01:56.871 --> 00:01:58.460
to what you have at
Microsoft Research

00:01:58.461 --> 00:02:00.090
but a bit different,

00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:01.620
of course. It's more companies.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:04.505
It's more universities,
and it's focused

00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:07.460
on things like continuous
delivery on architecting,

00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:09.109
on measurement and management,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.170
and customer data and ecosystems,

00:02:12.171 --> 00:02:15.480
working with
companies like Volvo,

00:02:15.481 --> 00:02:17.880
Grundfos that make use of pumps.

00:02:17.881 --> 00:02:21.375
Here a person who is
working with maps,

00:02:21.376 --> 00:02:24.030
Bosch enzimas you
know, of course.

00:02:24.031 --> 00:02:27.995
Not. So, why we

00:02:27.996 --> 00:02:30.160
so excited about working
with a software center is

00:02:30.161 --> 00:02:32.650
because gives us
the opportunity to work in

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:36.505
a very different way than
most academic research is done.

00:02:36.506 --> 00:02:38.620
So, since this is
a formalized agreement,

00:02:38.621 --> 00:02:40.090
we have the possibility to go to

00:02:40.091 --> 00:02:41.990
one company, test the idea,

00:02:41.991 --> 00:02:44.050
and after a while go
to another company,

00:02:44.051 --> 00:02:45.910
test the idea and
improve that further,

00:02:45.911 --> 00:02:48.850
and then move on to
more and more companies.

00:02:48.851 --> 00:02:51.205
So this is how we basically do.

00:02:51.206 --> 00:02:54.370
We take for instance the
code churning that Nacci

00:02:54.371 --> 00:02:58.030
and some of your colleagues
have written a while back,

00:02:58.031 --> 00:02:59.530
and we apply that at Ericsson,

00:02:59.531 --> 00:03:01.270
and then a colleague
of mine Robert

00:03:01.271 --> 00:03:03.065
has introduced me to heat-maps,

00:03:03.066 --> 00:03:04.590
so we use heat-maps to visualize

00:03:04.591 --> 00:03:07.190
churning in the
large product-based.

00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:11.325
After a while, we took
that idea from Ericsson to SAP,

00:03:11.326 --> 00:03:13.600
where they basically
said that well,

00:03:13.601 --> 00:03:15.310
if you can visualize that,

00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:17.710
maybe you can also predict
the coming changes,

00:03:17.711 --> 00:03:20.555
or predict changes that should
be done but were not done.

00:03:20.556 --> 00:03:23.610
So we did this that we
call the change waves,

00:03:23.611 --> 00:03:25.630
and based on that when we
took it to Volvo trucks,

00:03:25.631 --> 00:03:28.500
we could also predict
the impact of

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:32.580
change waves and that code
churning on the quality,

00:03:32.581 --> 00:03:34.890
both of the quality of the code,

00:03:34.891 --> 00:03:36.615
but also quality
of the final truck

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:37.830
that is driving around.

00:03:37.831 --> 00:03:41.985
Then in the end we took
it to the Volvo cars.

00:03:41.986 --> 00:03:44.730
It could actually bring
in both the defects and

00:03:44.731 --> 00:03:48.120
requirements and code
into one dashboard.

00:03:48.121 --> 00:03:50.010
It could predict
the wave of changes in

00:03:50.011 --> 00:03:52.770
the requirements that would
spread to the source code,

00:03:52.771 --> 00:03:54.510
that would spread
to the test cases,

00:03:54.511 --> 00:03:56.205
that would spread to the quality.

00:03:56.206 --> 00:03:58.910
So we could basically predict

00:03:58.911 --> 00:04:03.090
the things about half a
year in advance. All right.

00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:05.915
This kind of work
resulted in roughly

00:04:05.916 --> 00:04:07.520
40,000 measurement systems that

00:04:07.521 --> 00:04:09.155
we built at different companies,

00:04:09.156 --> 00:04:11.270
and I will introduce you to what

00:04:11.271 --> 00:04:13.370
a measurement system is later on.

00:04:13.371 --> 00:04:15.280
But you could see this as

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:19.910
a gadget which
visualizes some sort

00:04:19.911 --> 00:04:22.640
of measurements that
are being processed in

00:04:22.641 --> 00:04:26.870
completely autonomous way
based on either big databases,

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:29.560
Excel, or BI behind it,

00:04:29.561 --> 00:04:32.639
that has mechanisms for
example self-healing,

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.280
so that those 40,000

00:04:34.281 --> 00:04:35.390
measurement systems if they

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:37.955
crash they could actually
repair themselves.

00:04:37.956 --> 00:04:40.880
So this infrastructure
of 40,000 people is

00:04:40.881 --> 00:04:45.220
maintained by roughly
2.5 percent per year,

00:04:45.221 --> 00:04:48.610
which is very little, given
amount of work that they do.

00:04:48.611 --> 00:04:51.960
So, thanks to
the self-healing that

00:04:51.961 --> 00:04:55.310
we did was that instead
of spending hours a week,

00:04:55.311 --> 00:04:57.670
they could spend hours a month on

00:04:57.671 --> 00:04:58.840
maintaining those things and then

00:04:58.841 --> 00:05:00.350
focus on new development.

00:05:00.351 --> 00:05:01.810
We have worked a lot with

00:05:01.811 --> 00:05:03.805
things about information quality.

00:05:03.806 --> 00:05:05.905
So if you see a number
at the very top,

00:05:05.906 --> 00:05:07.120
the defect backlog eight,

00:05:07.121 --> 00:05:09.220
and you see the red dot,
that red dot is

00:05:09.221 --> 00:05:12.260
actually showing if you can
trust the number or not.

00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:13.990
So even if though we
can mathematically

00:05:13.991 --> 00:05:15.640
get the number of defects,

00:05:15.641 --> 00:05:16.960
there could be a lot
of properties for

00:05:16.961 --> 00:05:20.155
instance the database may
not have been updated,

00:05:20.156 --> 00:05:22.810
the defects are
outside of the limits

00:05:22.811 --> 00:05:26.305
so even if you see eight
maybe you could expect 20,

00:05:26.306 --> 00:05:28.910
so where are the other missing
defect and so on.

00:05:28.911 --> 00:05:31.355
So we build a lot of
tech into the data,

00:05:31.356 --> 00:05:32.800
that gives the stakeholder some

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:34.520
understanding if he or she could

00:05:34.521 --> 00:05:37.780
trust that information on it.

00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:41.270
Then we of course have
estimations of things,

00:05:41.271 --> 00:05:43.010
for instance we
apply those change

00:05:43.011 --> 00:05:45.785
waves analysis at Volvo.

00:05:45.786 --> 00:05:48.440
So we could predict
if a change in

00:05:48.441 --> 00:05:51.630
a standard of auto start,

00:05:51.631 --> 00:05:54.845
how much that would cost to
implement in a new Volvo car,

00:05:54.846 --> 00:05:58.235
and that analysis was usually
done during few months,

00:05:58.236 --> 00:06:00.485
but thanks to Darko and his work,

00:06:00.486 --> 00:06:02.480
he could do it in days.

00:06:02.481 --> 00:06:03.780
They could press a button,

00:06:03.781 --> 00:06:05.670
get a lot of information,

00:06:05.671 --> 00:06:07.190
build a model, and
then they could just

00:06:07.191 --> 00:06:10.110
evaluate if that model
was correct or not.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.290
Then of course what we also
did is we could also apply

00:06:14.291 --> 00:06:15.880
exactly the same
things to look at

00:06:15.881 --> 00:06:17.680
the quality of requirements,

00:06:17.681 --> 00:06:20.050
so we could pinpoint
requirements that did not

00:06:20.051 --> 00:06:23.995
fulfill specific criteria
very early during the review.

00:06:23.996 --> 00:06:25.240
So if you could review

00:06:25.241 --> 00:06:28.610
a specification with
100,000 requirements,

00:06:28.611 --> 00:06:30.760
instead of spending
weeks of reading all of

00:06:30.761 --> 00:06:32.800
them and trying to
build a mental model,

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:34.780
that too would allow
you to focus on say

00:06:34.781 --> 00:06:39.025
200 requirements that
really need your attention.

00:06:39.026 --> 00:06:40.360
Maybe they are not consistent,

00:06:40.361 --> 00:06:41.380
or they're not coherent,

00:06:41.381 --> 00:06:45.100
or they need some extra
information or maybe they're

00:06:45.101 --> 00:06:50.680
just wrong, planing usage.

00:06:50.681 --> 00:06:53.155
So that is what we
did quite manually.

00:06:53.156 --> 00:06:56.200
Now what we doing right
now is focusing a lot of

00:06:56.201 --> 00:06:57.670
applying this machine learning

00:06:57.671 --> 00:07:00.250
techniques and artificial
intelligence and measurement.

00:07:00.251 --> 00:07:02.470
So, quite normally, when you have

00:07:02.471 --> 00:07:05.920
a measurement program
in a company,

00:07:05.921 --> 00:07:07.420
you take a lot of data and try to

00:07:07.421 --> 00:07:09.310
build up some decision support,

00:07:09.311 --> 00:07:11.320
you could see this as
a BI for instance, right?

00:07:11.321 --> 00:07:12.730
It's an example of it,

00:07:12.731 --> 00:07:15.790
but that requires a lot
of manual effort,

00:07:15.791 --> 00:07:17.920
to find out what
the questions are,

00:07:17.921 --> 00:07:19.660
what does the stakeholder want,

00:07:19.661 --> 00:07:21.520
who is the stakeholder.

00:07:21.521 --> 00:07:23.975
What their goals are and

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:25.745
what kind of measurements you do.

00:07:25.746 --> 00:07:27.525
But if we apply,

00:07:27.526 --> 00:07:29.665
for example, machine
learning in our AI,

00:07:29.666 --> 00:07:31.410
those systems could
basically tell

00:07:31.411 --> 00:07:34.410
you what is it that
you need to focus on.

00:07:34.411 --> 00:07:37.690
So, in a case of a
company who has 40,000

00:07:37.691 --> 00:07:38.890
measurement systems and

00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:41.240
maybe hundreds of
thousands of measurements,

00:07:41.241 --> 00:07:44.060
the AI could pick out
those measurements that are

00:07:44.061 --> 00:07:46.560
off limits today and
focus your attention.

00:07:46.561 --> 00:07:50.010
So, if you for instance focus
too much on quality and

00:07:50.011 --> 00:07:51.970
then forget that
the important part

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:53.855
is to shift the features
in your product,

00:07:53.856 --> 00:07:55.330
the measurement system will start

00:07:55.331 --> 00:07:57.230
flagging that you're
lagging behind in

00:07:57.231 --> 00:07:59.710
the feature development
or vice versa if you

00:07:59.711 --> 00:08:02.610
just focus on new development
but don't test on it,

00:08:02.611 --> 00:08:04.600
this AI will tell you that
now you should really

00:08:04.601 --> 00:08:05.690
focus on testing because

00:08:05.691 --> 00:08:07.610
your test progress
is slowing down,

00:08:07.611 --> 00:08:12.260
and because of that they
suffer from quality and so on.

00:08:12.261 --> 00:08:15.860
Could also gave you a lot
of simulations on that.

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:18.740
Where we came from is that
we started with working

00:08:18.741 --> 00:08:21.195
on the manual predictions
long time ago,

00:08:21.196 --> 00:08:23.520
we build a lot of prediction
models together with

00:08:23.521 --> 00:08:25.800
Ericsson and then we found out

00:08:25.801 --> 00:08:28.190
though those predictions are
very good but they are very

00:08:28.191 --> 00:08:29.840
unreliable in many cases

00:08:29.841 --> 00:08:31.515
because they are very
product-specific,

00:08:31.516 --> 00:08:34.390
it's very difficult to
build a uniform model that

00:08:34.391 --> 00:08:35.830
will predict number of defects

00:08:35.831 --> 00:08:37.690
in any product in the world.

00:08:37.691 --> 00:08:39.800
So, what we came up
with was that we could

00:08:39.801 --> 00:08:42.020
automate certain
quality checks look

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:44.040
for example of if

00:08:44.041 --> 00:08:46.160
the variables are metrics
or outside of limits,

00:08:46.161 --> 00:08:47.990
and if they are outside of
limits we could tell you

00:08:47.991 --> 00:08:49.830
that okay this is the
model that you can use.

00:08:49.831 --> 00:08:51.210
You will get a number
but you shouldn't

00:08:51.211 --> 00:08:52.760
trust the number because

00:08:52.761 --> 00:08:54.310
the values are outside of

00:08:54.311 --> 00:08:57.350
the range that are usually
predicted for that.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:01.185
We focus on mostly
things like defect,

00:09:01.186 --> 00:09:04.440
on faults or failures in
the product and then we

00:09:04.441 --> 00:09:06.150
said that that's of
course a very good thing

00:09:06.151 --> 00:09:07.840
but it's a very reactive thing.

00:09:07.841 --> 00:09:08.950
So, we moved onto

00:09:08.951 --> 00:09:12.245
cold stability
visualization where we used

00:09:12.246 --> 00:09:14.860
cold churning and basically

00:09:14.861 --> 00:09:16.280
characteristics of cold churning

00:09:16.281 --> 00:09:18.090
to make prediction models.

00:09:18.091 --> 00:09:21.540
Then we could build up things

00:09:21.541 --> 00:09:24.720
like robust measurement progress
based on that so be able

00:09:24.721 --> 00:09:26.460
to predict and warn

00:09:26.461 --> 00:09:29.590
stakeholders a few months
before something happened that

00:09:29.591 --> 00:09:30.750
you're going to
have problems with

00:09:30.751 --> 00:09:32.250
this product or you're
going to have problems with

00:09:32.251 --> 00:09:33.650
this module or you're
going to have problems

00:09:33.651 --> 00:09:36.760
with that type of customers.

00:09:36.761 --> 00:09:39.040
What we do today is we do

00:09:39.041 --> 00:09:40.950
this AI based software
analytics when

00:09:40.951 --> 00:09:43.050
we building a lot of intelligence

00:09:43.051 --> 00:09:45.180
in the measurement called base.

00:09:45.181 --> 00:09:48.030
So instead of
spending years and months

00:09:48.031 --> 00:09:50.595
to analyze your data,

00:09:50.596 --> 00:09:52.490
the AI gives you the answers

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.745
directly by itself so to say.

00:09:54.746 --> 00:09:58.355
That brings me to how
do we do that, right?

00:09:58.356 --> 00:10:03.600
So, if we take companies
like Ericsson or

00:10:03.601 --> 00:10:06.585
Volvo or any other company

00:10:06.586 --> 00:10:09.225
they have something they
call measurement program.

00:10:09.226 --> 00:10:10.440
So, they have stakeholders,

00:10:10.441 --> 00:10:13.210
they have information needs,
they measure things,

00:10:13.211 --> 00:10:15.520
they measure product,
they measure processes,

00:10:15.521 --> 00:10:19.165
productivity, quality
and aspects like that.

00:10:19.166 --> 00:10:21.820
They tried to put them as
key performance indicators

00:10:21.821 --> 00:10:25.600
to put on big monitors and
show how good are we today,

00:10:25.601 --> 00:10:26.790
how many customers did we

00:10:26.791 --> 00:10:29.710
get an how many
customers do we lose?

00:10:29.711 --> 00:10:32.195
But those are very reactive,

00:10:32.196 --> 00:10:34.275
they are set up by
people who have

00:10:34.276 --> 00:10:36.210
domain knowledge and these people

00:10:36.211 --> 00:10:38.770
based all of that on
their experience.

00:10:38.771 --> 00:10:41.700
But as you know the world
is not built on experience,

00:10:41.701 --> 00:10:43.305
the world is changing
all the time.

00:10:43.306 --> 00:10:45.220
So, if you build things based on

00:10:45.221 --> 00:10:48.320
your experience quite often
you can miss something.

00:10:48.321 --> 00:10:49.890
What we do is we provide

00:10:49.891 --> 00:10:53.240
more decision support to

00:10:53.241 --> 00:10:56.185
exchange experience
with some new ideas.

00:10:56.186 --> 00:10:58.220
So we build for example

00:10:58.221 --> 00:11:00.980
algorithms based on decision
trees to come up with

00:11:00.981 --> 00:11:03.580
new metrics that will

00:11:03.581 --> 00:11:05.760
tell you that this is
an aspect that you don't

00:11:05.761 --> 00:11:07.890
really cover based on

00:11:07.891 --> 00:11:10.995
applied statistics
and things like that.

00:11:10.996 --> 00:11:14.685
We do this quite often,

00:11:14.686 --> 00:11:18.740
we have a repository of over
2,000 different metrics that

00:11:18.741 --> 00:11:21.610
we constantly increase and

00:11:21.611 --> 00:11:24.630
show different aspect
of what we do.

00:11:24.631 --> 00:11:29.700
We have this self-healing
infrastructure that does it.

00:11:29.701 --> 00:11:31.950
Imagine if you are processing

00:11:31.951 --> 00:11:33.460
this amount of data you cannot

00:11:33.461 --> 00:11:35.520
really manually monitor that

00:11:35.521 --> 00:11:37.190
everything is right all the time.

00:11:37.191 --> 00:11:42.150
So what we have is, we
have this systems that do

00:11:42.151 --> 00:11:44.305
a lot of magic behind the scenes

00:11:44.306 --> 00:11:47.480
looking at for instance
coloniality of data,

00:11:47.481 --> 00:11:50.075
so the mathematical
properties of the data,

00:11:50.076 --> 00:11:53.340
it looks at the processes
behind the data for instance,

00:11:53.341 --> 00:11:56.330
has people been reporting
defects into the system,

00:11:56.331 --> 00:11:58.395
have test cases being executed,

00:11:58.396 --> 00:12:00.310
have code being checked

00:12:00.311 --> 00:12:02.875
into the repositories,
all kinds of things?

00:12:02.876 --> 00:12:05.090
Based on those aspects,

00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:07.525
we could tell that for instance

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:10.700
something has happened in
the data that for example,

00:12:10.701 --> 00:12:12.330
people have stopped checking

00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:14.220
in the code into Git repository.

00:12:14.221 --> 00:12:16.450
Meaning that one has to look into

00:12:16.451 --> 00:12:19.570
that and decide if
that's something

00:12:19.571 --> 00:12:22.080
that we should ignore or
is that something that

00:12:22.081 --> 00:12:25.395
is not required. Yes.

00:12:25.396 --> 00:12:27.840
&gt;&gt; What is self-healing
measurement?

00:12:27.841 --> 00:12:29.895
What is that practicing mean?

00:12:29.896 --> 00:12:31.735
&gt;&gt; So, in practice
that means that

00:12:31.736 --> 00:12:34.420
sometimes if you
measure something,

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.105
say the closest example if

00:12:38.106 --> 00:12:41.030
someone doesn't report a defect,

00:12:41.031 --> 00:12:43.510
you may get a division by
zero somewhere, right?

00:12:43.511 --> 00:12:46.200
In normal cases you could
visualize that idea was

00:12:46.201 --> 00:12:50.380
an infinity or error or zero

00:12:50.381 --> 00:12:53.700
but even if that's a number it

00:12:53.701 --> 00:12:57.645
doesn't mean that it's
a correct number.

00:12:57.646 --> 00:12:59.490
So, what the self-healing does

00:12:59.491 --> 00:13:01.340
is that it looks
into why did we get

00:13:01.341 --> 00:13:03.530
the missing defect and if that's

00:13:03.531 --> 00:13:05.880
for example a missing connection
to the database,

00:13:05.881 --> 00:13:07.520
it will try to pull

00:13:07.521 --> 00:13:10.860
the database few more times
to try to get the data,

00:13:10.861 --> 00:13:13.475
and maybe the database
needs to be restarted.

00:13:13.476 --> 00:13:14.890
So, the self-healing mechanism

00:13:14.891 --> 00:13:16.090
knows that it's a database,

00:13:16.091 --> 00:13:18.070
it could be restarted
that way so it restarts

00:13:18.071 --> 00:13:21.480
the database and that's a lot
of those kind of checks.

00:13:21.481 --> 00:13:23.790
In most cases can fix the problem

00:13:23.791 --> 00:13:25.810
with the missing data in

00:13:25.811 --> 00:13:31.060
a much more meaningful way
than just having zero on that.

00:13:31.061 --> 00:13:33.130
&gt;&gt;  Who hosted by
a hardware operating

00:13:33.131 --> 00:13:36.485
system applications or
just an application?

00:13:36.486 --> 00:13:38.260
&gt;&gt; From your perspective that

00:13:38.261 --> 00:13:40.670
will be mostly applications.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:50.080
We don't do operating system
we don't go that deep.

00:13:50.081 --> 00:13:52.440
All right let me move on then.

00:13:52.441 --> 00:13:55.690
That is important with
this because this is

00:13:55.691 --> 00:13:58.180
how a measurement system
could be visualized, right?

00:13:58.181 --> 00:14:01.065
So, it's everything
from mobile phone apps,

00:14:01.066 --> 00:14:04.200
gadgets in all times to websites,

00:14:04.201 --> 00:14:09.775
to builds in continuous
integration or things that

00:14:09.776 --> 00:14:12.650
based on sonar cube or
on Tableau dashboards

00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:16.180
or Power BI or kinds of systems,

00:14:16.181 --> 00:14:19.455
but that's just a visualization
at the final step, right.

00:14:19.456 --> 00:14:21.955
What we do with the AI or

00:14:21.956 --> 00:14:23.390
machine learning
is that we change

00:14:23.391 --> 00:14:25.330
those visualizations
automatically.

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:28.055
So, in the old days one
could see that this was a,

00:14:28.056 --> 00:14:30.420
let's take this picture, that was

00:14:30.421 --> 00:14:31.510
a measurement system showing

00:14:31.511 --> 00:14:33.960
bottlenecks in software
development program.

00:14:33.961 --> 00:14:37.070
So, it was divided by phases
and what could look at

00:14:37.071 --> 00:14:38.980
different activities
and the throughput

00:14:38.981 --> 00:14:41.255
and cues and flow in the system,

00:14:41.256 --> 00:14:43.365
but as you see that's
quite cluttered.

00:14:43.366 --> 00:14:44.990
It's a many different diagrams

00:14:44.991 --> 00:14:47.595
and don't really know
where to look for.

00:14:47.596 --> 00:14:49.970
So, what we do today
instead of that is,

00:14:49.971 --> 00:14:52.385
we take away
that picture and then

00:14:52.386 --> 00:14:56.150
let the machine learning
look at decision trees and

00:14:56.151 --> 00:15:00.200
regression models to
look at the dependencies

00:15:00.201 --> 00:15:02.270
between different metrics
and then based on

00:15:02.271 --> 00:15:03.620
the deviations from dependencies

00:15:03.621 --> 00:15:05.000
we can basically say that for

00:15:05.001 --> 00:15:06.320
instance now you have

00:15:06.321 --> 00:15:08.405
a lot of queue in
the defect handling.

00:15:08.406 --> 00:15:10.100
So, we visualize only this part

00:15:10.101 --> 00:15:11.970
and so you should focus on that.

00:15:11.971 --> 00:15:15.065
But if you have a little defects

00:15:15.066 --> 00:15:17.270
but a lot of for example,

00:15:17.271 --> 00:15:20.125
a lot of development and
a lot of integration,

00:15:20.126 --> 00:15:21.900
we could visualize
something that's not on

00:15:21.901 --> 00:15:24.120
this diagram that is a testing.

00:15:24.121 --> 00:15:26.395
Saying that okay you
have developing a lot,

00:15:26.396 --> 00:15:30.075
things are happening but
we don't see any defects.

00:15:30.076 --> 00:15:32.010
Meaning that there must
be something wrong

00:15:32.011 --> 00:15:33.695
with your testing so
look at your testing.

00:15:33.696 --> 00:15:36.105
Are you testing enough? Are
you testing the right thing?

00:15:36.106 --> 00:15:38.890
Are you may be
missing test cases?

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:42.645
Try to find out where are
the things that are not

00:15:42.646 --> 00:15:45.270
really visualize or
not really there. Yes.

00:15:45.271 --> 00:15:46.980
&gt;&gt; How do you go about training

00:15:46.981 --> 00:15:51.330
the models that direct
people's attention?

00:15:51.331 --> 00:15:53.640
&gt;&gt; What we do is that at
the beginning we train

00:15:53.641 --> 00:15:56.460
that on historical
data but then we

00:15:56.461 --> 00:16:00.630
have a model that over time looks

00:16:00.631 --> 00:16:03.510
at when we should stop using

00:16:03.511 --> 00:16:05.160
this particular trained model

00:16:05.161 --> 00:16:06.705
and should we train the data.

00:16:06.706 --> 00:16:09.300
So, we have an assemble
learner that over

00:16:09.301 --> 00:16:12.365
time trains the model.

00:16:12.366 --> 00:16:14.940
We basically fuse
on decision trees

00:16:14.941 --> 00:16:16.585
and the random forests.

00:16:16.586 --> 00:16:18.510
Over time when we
see that this model

00:16:18.511 --> 00:16:21.330
is getting a bit off charge,

00:16:21.331 --> 00:16:23.060
then we take away
that and we train

00:16:23.061 --> 00:16:24.950
that on new model
continuously so to say.

00:16:24.951 --> 00:16:26.750
&gt;&gt; So this was supervised
learning, right?

00:16:26.751 --> 00:16:30.260
So, where does labels come from?

00:16:30.261 --> 00:16:32.670
I mean at some point you
must have a label that

00:16:32.671 --> 00:16:36.125
says this metric is
important at this moment.

00:16:36.126 --> 00:16:38.500
&gt;&gt; So we have two things,
one it comes from

00:16:38.501 --> 00:16:42.460
for example, information quality.

00:16:42.461 --> 00:16:45.060
When we notice that
sometimes for instance

00:16:45.061 --> 00:16:48.700
this data it's been off
the charts for example.

00:16:48.701 --> 00:16:51.225
That we expect the defects
to be no more than say

00:16:51.226 --> 00:16:55.680
10 per day but now we
just have 20 every week.

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:57.930
So, that lamp is
going to go red a bit

00:16:57.931 --> 00:16:58.950
more and more and then someone

00:16:58.951 --> 00:17:00.350
looks at that and say, "Okay,

00:17:00.351 --> 00:17:02.540
should we retrain the
model or should we go to

00:17:02.541 --> 00:17:05.245
the team and say look what's
wrong with your testing?"

00:17:05.246 --> 00:17:07.280
So, that's one aspect.
The other aspect is that if

00:17:07.281 --> 00:17:10.890
we get a new data
type for instance,

00:17:10.891 --> 00:17:12.770
or new metric or we could

00:17:12.771 --> 00:17:14.780
call it the new
feature for the model,

00:17:14.781 --> 00:17:16.550
then we tried to retrain
the model based on

00:17:16.551 --> 00:17:19.460
some simple algorithm and
then we use active learning

00:17:19.461 --> 00:17:23.675
when for example
random forest give us

00:17:23.676 --> 00:17:25.670
a high probability that

00:17:25.671 --> 00:17:29.435
the algorithm doesn't know
where to classify that aspect.

00:17:29.436 --> 00:17:32.090
Then the quality manager or
team leader has to press

00:17:32.091 --> 00:17:34.960
the button "Is that
okay" or "Not okay. "

00:17:34.961 --> 00:17:36.750
For the beginning we'll learn

00:17:36.751 --> 00:17:38.930
using basic active learning.

00:17:38.931 --> 00:17:40.235
So those two things,

00:17:40.236 --> 00:17:42.110
information quality
and active learning.

00:17:42.111 --> 00:17:42.790
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:42.791 --> 00:17:44.350
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:44.351 --> 00:17:45.770
All right.

00:17:45.771 --> 00:17:49.320
So, if it comes

00:17:49.321 --> 00:17:53.545
to this information quality,
we'll look at two things.

00:17:53.546 --> 00:17:57.085
So, the one I talked about is
the more internal quality,

00:17:57.086 --> 00:17:59.040
when we look at the kind

00:17:59.041 --> 00:18:01.030
of mathematical
properties of the data.

00:18:01.031 --> 00:18:03.500
But we also look at
the external quality.

00:18:03.501 --> 00:18:05.990
What does the stakeholder
think about the data?

00:18:05.991 --> 00:18:08.250
So, this situation when

00:18:08.251 --> 00:18:11.490
the metric is outside
of the limits,

00:18:11.491 --> 00:18:15.795
and it's all based on
the ice of 5939 standard,

00:18:15.796 --> 00:18:18.590
which is a standard showing how

00:18:18.591 --> 00:18:20.930
the information in the
measurement system flows

00:18:20.931 --> 00:18:23.710
basically from the attributes or

00:18:23.711 --> 00:18:27.085
products until the information
needs of the stakeholder,

00:18:27.086 --> 00:18:28.900
how that thing is build up,

00:18:28.901 --> 00:18:32.150
and this whole monitoring
of information quality.

00:18:32.151 --> 00:18:35.505
Now this is just a set of checks.

00:18:35.506 --> 00:18:38.255
This whole monitoring is based
on checking properties of

00:18:38.256 --> 00:18:41.945
every step of
the calculation to some.

00:18:41.946 --> 00:18:44.360
That's a basic idea. Then if

00:18:44.361 --> 00:18:46.770
something goes wrong like
this one for instance

00:18:46.771 --> 00:18:50.060
that this check says that
the raw data was not

00:18:50.061 --> 00:18:51.710
updated for example someone did

00:18:51.711 --> 00:18:54.005
not report any defects yesterday,

00:18:54.006 --> 00:18:59.120
then that will be more for
information system designer,

00:18:59.121 --> 00:19:01.660
but for the stakeholder,

00:19:01.661 --> 00:19:03.090
we'll just show red

00:19:03.091 --> 00:19:05.310
without saying exactly what
is the problem.

00:19:05.311 --> 00:19:07.180
Because the stakeholder think

00:19:07.181 --> 00:19:09.405
about for instance
product manager,

00:19:09.406 --> 00:19:11.290
a product manager
doesn't know exactly

00:19:11.291 --> 00:19:14.570
which raw data is used and
how that is used and so on.

00:19:14.571 --> 00:19:17.350
So he only gets the indication
for this moment,

00:19:17.351 --> 00:19:19.135
you cannot really
trust that number,

00:19:19.136 --> 00:19:21.205
but the metric design
he can go in.

00:19:21.206 --> 00:19:24.320
He also gets that number
on his or her screen,

00:19:24.321 --> 00:19:27.040
and he can go in and check
exactly which data was

00:19:27.041 --> 00:19:31.820
not updated and what
to do about that.

00:19:31.821 --> 00:19:35.130
Right? As I said,

00:19:35.131 --> 00:19:38.500
it's based on the ice
of 5939 standard.

00:19:38.501 --> 00:19:40.650
How we do machine learning in

00:19:40.651 --> 00:19:42.630
that is that we add a lot of

00:19:42.631 --> 00:19:46.560
things around that basic idea
of checks, right?

00:19:46.561 --> 00:19:47.780
So if you have a lot of checks,

00:19:47.781 --> 00:19:49.270
you could use
the checks as labels

00:19:49.271 --> 00:19:51.985
for training of Machine
Learning to some extent,

00:19:51.986 --> 00:19:54.720
and we basically do
this data collection.

00:19:54.721 --> 00:19:56.165
So that was what you saw

00:19:56.166 --> 00:19:57.980
simplified on
the right-hand side,

00:19:57.981 --> 00:20:00.710
and on the left-hand side
is the other things

00:20:00.711 --> 00:20:03.655
that are connected
to the organization.

00:20:03.656 --> 00:20:05.410
For example how do we know that

00:20:05.411 --> 00:20:07.210
a specific KPI is

00:20:07.211 --> 00:20:09.575
really important for
that particular organization?

00:20:09.576 --> 00:20:11.890
How do we know that Company X

00:20:11.891 --> 00:20:14.520
has to focus on speed
and not on quality?

00:20:14.521 --> 00:20:16.815
Wait, what does it say,

00:20:16.816 --> 00:20:18.635
and then we'll look
at for example of

00:20:18.636 --> 00:20:20.040
reference models from from

00:20:20.041 --> 00:20:21.935
other industries or
other companies,

00:20:21.936 --> 00:20:23.325
and try to compare them,

00:20:23.326 --> 00:20:27.290
and see that company X is
much slower than company B,

00:20:27.291 --> 00:20:30.080
and then company B
is in the same area,

00:20:30.081 --> 00:20:31.665
but they could develop a lot

00:20:31.666 --> 00:20:34.190
faster or integrate a lot faster,

00:20:34.191 --> 00:20:35.990
and there comes the strength of

00:20:35.991 --> 00:20:37.960
the collaboration we have
in the software Central

00:20:37.961 --> 00:20:39.950
have different companies that

00:20:39.951 --> 00:20:41.230
are quite open to each other.

00:20:41.231 --> 00:20:42.570
So we could compare
and they could

00:20:42.571 --> 00:20:44.505
learn from each other and
they could say, "look,

00:20:44.506 --> 00:20:48.170
in our model we could integrate
within say half an hour,

00:20:48.171 --> 00:20:49.600
and you can integrate
with and you

00:20:49.601 --> 00:20:51.310
need a week to integrate
your software.

00:20:51.311 --> 00:20:58.020
So, there is a possibility
for improvement on that."

00:20:58.021 --> 00:21:00.620
Thanks to all this work

00:21:00.621 --> 00:21:02.530
that we did based on
the culture learning,

00:21:02.531 --> 00:21:03.630
and from you for instance,

00:21:03.631 --> 00:21:05.650
we could teach the mothers to

00:21:05.651 --> 00:21:08.200
find where the problems in
the code could actually

00:21:08.201 --> 00:21:10.420
pop up based on the dependencies

00:21:10.421 --> 00:21:13.525
between the cold churns
and for example testing.

00:21:13.526 --> 00:21:19.230
So we'll have a regression
model that shows where are

00:21:19.231 --> 00:21:20.640
the emission test cases or

00:21:20.641 --> 00:21:22.810
which test cases we
could suggest to

00:21:22.811 --> 00:21:27.520
execute based on a change in
the specific code module.

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:36.170
We'll also work on
the measurements under quality

00:21:36.171 --> 00:21:37.680
from the perspective
of the impact

00:21:37.681 --> 00:21:39.395
that they have on the company.

00:21:39.396 --> 00:21:41.710
So, for example, we look at

00:21:41.711 --> 00:21:43.870
the entire measurement program,

00:21:43.871 --> 00:21:46.160
and an entire measurement
program could have

00:21:46.161 --> 00:21:51.190
maybe up to 500 measurements
in them or indicators,

00:21:51.191 --> 00:21:53.090
and we'll look and
try to identify

00:21:53.091 --> 00:21:55.395
which of the indicators
are the leading ones.

00:21:55.396 --> 00:21:57.035
Say, if you could only pick

00:21:57.036 --> 00:21:58.855
10 percent of your indicators.

00:21:58.856 --> 00:22:01.125
So, 50 indicators out of 500,

00:22:01.126 --> 00:22:02.615
which would you pick?

00:22:02.616 --> 00:22:05.265
Which 50 are
the most important ones?

00:22:05.266 --> 00:22:07.980
You can look of course at

00:22:07.981 --> 00:22:10.430
the mathematical properties
like colinearity

00:22:10.431 --> 00:22:15.275
and codependency and try
to predict one another.

00:22:15.276 --> 00:22:17.710
But what you could
lose is of course

00:22:17.711 --> 00:22:20.680
the slight deviations
when for instance,

00:22:20.681 --> 00:22:22.950
you have two indicators
and they are in 99 percent

00:22:22.951 --> 00:22:25.480
of the weeks they
show the same status,

00:22:25.481 --> 00:22:27.955
but this one percent of
the weeks when they actually

00:22:27.956 --> 00:22:29.350
show different status and that's

00:22:29.351 --> 00:22:31.425
o percent when it's
really important.

00:22:31.426 --> 00:22:33.930
So we do a lot of work

00:22:33.931 --> 00:22:36.040
with the stakeholders
when they provide us

00:22:36.041 --> 00:22:39.080
with different weights of

00:22:39.081 --> 00:22:41.080
different measurements
or measurement aspects

00:22:41.081 --> 00:22:42.185
in the measurement program,

00:22:42.186 --> 00:22:43.865
and based on those aspects

00:22:43.866 --> 00:22:45.660
what we do is we could also build

00:22:45.661 --> 00:22:47.140
a model saying that even if

00:22:47.141 --> 00:22:49.585
mathematically two indicators
are correlated,

00:22:49.586 --> 00:22:51.110
the important thing is to

00:22:51.111 --> 00:22:52.980
show when they deviate
from one another,

00:22:52.981 --> 00:22:54.260
that sort of one percent

00:22:54.261 --> 00:22:58.810
when the correlation
is not perfect.

00:22:58.811 --> 00:23:01.180
Again helps us a lot

00:23:01.181 --> 00:23:04.580
if we're working with
multiple companies,

00:23:04.581 --> 00:23:07.890
when we do a lot of work
with different sectors,

00:23:07.891 --> 00:23:10.340
different types of stakeholders,

00:23:10.341 --> 00:23:12.910
and different type of metrics,

00:23:12.911 --> 00:23:14.970
and types of metrics for example,

00:23:14.971 --> 00:23:19.870
this is our metric
portfolio summary.

00:23:19.871 --> 00:23:22.460
The areas which we use,

00:23:22.461 --> 00:23:27.170
for example we have
Cloud computing measurements.

00:23:27.171 --> 00:23:28.970
Things like anything from

00:23:28.971 --> 00:23:31.485
stability of the cloud
environment to

00:23:31.486 --> 00:23:32.960
complexity of the code that is

00:23:32.961 --> 00:23:35.610
behind the cloud execution,

00:23:35.611 --> 00:23:37.295
scalability and so on.

00:23:37.296 --> 00:23:41.150
But also project
metrics for example how

00:23:41.151 --> 00:23:45.270
fast we deploy new features
on their customer site?

00:23:45.271 --> 00:23:46.655
How fast we test?

00:23:46.656 --> 00:23:48.350
How many reviews we do?

00:23:48.351 --> 00:23:49.730
We look at the product,

00:23:49.731 --> 00:23:51.785
we look at organizational
performance,

00:23:51.786 --> 00:23:55.850
for instance how fast can
a feature go from an idea

00:23:55.851 --> 00:23:57.930
to actually implement
that feature

00:23:57.931 --> 00:24:00.345
and shipped to a customer?

00:24:00.346 --> 00:24:02.710
Which is important in areas

00:24:02.711 --> 00:24:05.120
like Telecom or automotive today.

00:24:05.121 --> 00:24:07.860
We look at the customer site for

00:24:07.861 --> 00:24:11.050
example a product delivery.

00:24:11.051 --> 00:24:12.680
How do we know that a customer is

00:24:12.681 --> 00:24:14.745
actually happy with
the product delivery?

00:24:14.746 --> 00:24:16.755
I know that you work
with that a lot,

00:24:16.756 --> 00:24:18.070
but in the areas

00:24:18.071 --> 00:24:21.300
when for example
automotive is not as easy,

00:24:21.301 --> 00:24:22.700
because you've shipped a car and

00:24:22.701 --> 00:24:24.030
then it's out there driving.

00:24:24.031 --> 00:24:26.030
So we don't really collect
the same kind of data since

00:24:26.031 --> 00:24:29.615
the web system not
connected all the time.

00:24:29.616 --> 00:24:32.365
So it's difficult to find
that kind of information.

00:24:32.366 --> 00:24:34.010
But then we have
models and things

00:24:34.011 --> 00:24:36.070
that help us out with this,

00:24:36.071 --> 00:24:37.990
and if you're interested I

00:24:37.991 --> 00:24:41.015
could show you more
about those metrics.

00:24:41.016 --> 00:24:43.740
They come mostly from
either different papers,

00:24:43.741 --> 00:24:45.100
or from the empirical

00:24:45.101 --> 00:24:47.580
evidence companies
that we work with.

00:24:47.581 --> 00:24:49.615
So, we'll look at what they
are measuring today and

00:24:49.616 --> 00:24:52.855
what they know what works
and what doesn't work,

00:24:52.856 --> 00:24:55.880
which could be quite a lot
of good evidence.

00:24:55.881 --> 00:25:01.810
Instead of predicting,

00:25:01.811 --> 00:25:03.535
previously we have
focused a lot on

00:25:03.536 --> 00:25:06.485
predicting the values of metric
for example the defects.

00:25:06.486 --> 00:25:08.970
What we wanted to predict
one the defects would come

00:25:08.971 --> 00:25:11.625
and how many would come
in a specific product?

00:25:11.626 --> 00:25:13.740
What we should do?
Where they would come?

00:25:13.741 --> 00:25:15.725
So the location of the defects.

00:25:15.726 --> 00:25:17.770
But right now, what we

00:25:17.771 --> 00:25:19.680
tried to predict this
we tried to predict

00:25:19.681 --> 00:25:27.260
the events rather than
things related to defect.

00:25:27.261 --> 00:25:29.810
So, the numbers are
no longer important,

00:25:29.811 --> 00:25:31.380
what is important is the trend,

00:25:31.381 --> 00:25:32.925
are we going to be better in

00:25:32.926 --> 00:25:35.430
a week or two or are
we going to be worse?

00:25:35.431 --> 00:25:40.200
Should we prepare for something
or we should not on it?

00:25:40.201 --> 00:25:42.450
Which is the entry point of

00:25:42.451 --> 00:25:44.705
course to all of the
mathematics behind this,

00:25:44.706 --> 00:25:49.015
but it's communicated in
much easier way so to say.

00:25:49.016 --> 00:25:53.600
So, this is showing a gadget
it's roughly 10 years old.

00:25:53.601 --> 00:25:56.090
Yeah, roughly to any source
but today we use

00:25:56.091 --> 00:25:59.815
mobile phone apps and we use
emails instead of gadgets.

00:25:59.816 --> 00:26:01.210
So the stakeholders don't have to

00:26:01.211 --> 00:26:02.690
look at anything at all,

00:26:02.691 --> 00:26:04.270
they will just get
an email when something

00:26:04.271 --> 00:26:06.475
is out of range or they can

00:26:06.476 --> 00:26:08.440
get an SMS or when

00:26:08.441 --> 00:26:11.200
they're playing in
a mobile phone on that.

00:26:11.201 --> 00:26:13.420
Which is a tremendous difference
because then we could

00:26:13.421 --> 00:26:16.020
monitor more and
more data and then

00:26:16.021 --> 00:26:17.845
for that we need this
machine learning

00:26:17.846 --> 00:26:21.475
and the algorithm to monitor

00:26:21.476 --> 00:26:23.950
and then we only support
the stakeholders when

00:26:23.951 --> 00:26:28.090
something is really required
and really needed for that.

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:32.145
Of course we use a lot of
mathematics behind that.

00:26:32.146 --> 00:26:36.495
So, very simple formulas
that are not based on,

00:26:36.496 --> 00:26:39.505
so this an old formula showing

00:26:39.506 --> 00:26:45.055
how many weeks are

00:26:45.056 --> 00:26:47.835
needed to ship the
software to a customer.

00:26:47.836 --> 00:26:52.625
So, in that case it's the work
we did with Ericsson.

00:26:52.626 --> 00:26:54.980
So, if a customer to
Ericsson calls and say,

00:26:54.981 --> 00:26:57.000
I want to have the
latest and greatest,

00:26:57.001 --> 00:26:59.130
they need to know
how many weeks they need to

00:26:59.131 --> 00:27:01.390
actually provide them with the

00:27:01.391 --> 00:27:04.385
latest and greatest
software on their site.

00:27:04.386 --> 00:27:08.560
Then they can also have
this on a mobile phone app,

00:27:08.561 --> 00:27:11.630
how that works in this.

00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:14.030
That was about eight years ago

00:27:14.031 --> 00:27:15.750
when we started
working with that,

00:27:15.751 --> 00:27:18.275
that was very good
because it showed very

00:27:18.276 --> 00:27:21.450
succeed and very compact way of

00:27:21.451 --> 00:27:24.020
showing the quality of

00:27:24.021 --> 00:27:27.845
the product and
the plan deployment of that.

00:27:27.846 --> 00:27:29.260
But that wasn't enough,

00:27:29.261 --> 00:27:31.305
what was required was to have

00:27:31.306 --> 00:27:33.480
a more prediction view of that.

00:27:33.481 --> 00:27:37.120
So, what we did in one of
the studies is that we actually

00:27:37.121 --> 00:27:41.110
used heatmaps to look at
the profile of a code.

00:27:41.111 --> 00:27:43.250
So we would like to
know where do people do

00:27:43.251 --> 00:27:46.180
changes in the code base
and how do they do that.

00:27:46.181 --> 00:27:48.115
You can see two patterns.

00:27:48.116 --> 00:27:51.764
In that example, this is
an agile software development,

00:27:51.765 --> 00:27:54.845
where you see
the intensity of the color

00:27:54.846 --> 00:27:57.160
basically says how much code was

00:27:57.161 --> 00:28:00.110
changed per a particular week
a particular module.

00:28:00.111 --> 00:28:02.410
As you see in an agile
development you have changes

00:28:02.411 --> 00:28:04.890
all over the place on that,

00:28:04.891 --> 00:28:07.170
but this is an example of

00:28:07.171 --> 00:28:08.780
a product which is more

00:28:08.781 --> 00:28:11.170
a platform versus
application-based,

00:28:11.171 --> 00:28:13.690
and you see that quite a lot that

00:28:13.691 --> 00:28:16.200
there is a group of people
working with the platform,

00:28:16.201 --> 00:28:20.145
and they do a lot of work all
the time as in those cases.

00:28:20.146 --> 00:28:22.020
Then you see fragments of

00:28:22.021 --> 00:28:24.885
the code which are basically
different applications,

00:28:24.886 --> 00:28:26.640
and there you see
a completely different patterns,

00:28:26.641 --> 00:28:30.895
you see not read all over
the place but you see

00:28:30.896 --> 00:28:32.400
blobs when they focus on

00:28:32.401 --> 00:28:34.150
a specific feature that is

00:28:34.151 --> 00:28:36.375
or specific application
in that area,

00:28:36.376 --> 00:28:37.900
and then they move
on to another one,

00:28:37.901 --> 00:28:39.870
and so on and so on.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:45.190
This way of visualizing
this was actually a big hit

00:28:45.191 --> 00:28:46.730
showing that you could predict

00:28:46.731 --> 00:28:48.815
things for example
missing updates.

00:28:48.816 --> 00:28:52.055
So, if we look at
the big middle of that heatmap,

00:28:52.056 --> 00:28:54.365
one can of course start
asking the question,

00:28:54.366 --> 00:28:56.295
why don't we do
anything in that area?

00:28:56.296 --> 00:28:58.160
What's the area for?

00:28:58.161 --> 00:29:00.340
If historically you build

00:29:00.341 --> 00:29:03.660
a model saying that there are
some changes in this area,

00:29:03.661 --> 00:29:06.775
and as you see that
those dots sometimes have

00:29:06.776 --> 00:29:10.160
this property that one
happens before the other.

00:29:10.161 --> 00:29:13.340
So you could actually
try to build a model.

00:29:13.341 --> 00:29:15.775
As I said the code change wave,

00:29:15.776 --> 00:29:17.585
saying that if always this module

00:29:17.586 --> 00:29:19.795
is changed the week
before that module,

00:29:19.796 --> 00:29:23.190
I mean, why don't we
have that change in

00:29:23.191 --> 00:29:24.355
some week so you could

00:29:24.356 --> 00:29:26.860
identify missing changes
for example, right?

00:29:26.861 --> 00:29:29.145
They don't have to be
missing changes but it's

00:29:29.146 --> 00:29:30.910
definitely an input for someone

00:29:30.911 --> 00:29:32.855
to look at that code
and say, "Look,

00:29:32.856 --> 00:29:35.430
if you have usually
make change to module B

00:29:35.431 --> 00:29:38.545
after you make a
change in module A,

00:29:38.546 --> 00:29:40.460
why haven't you
done at this time?"

00:29:40.461 --> 00:29:43.605
That kind of way of
visualizing code also

00:29:43.606 --> 00:29:46.620
helps the teams to
prioritize test cases.

00:29:46.621 --> 00:29:49.890
So they could look at
this model and say, "So,

00:29:49.891 --> 00:29:51.970
now in this particular week we

00:29:51.971 --> 00:29:55.120
work very much in
those two modules,

00:29:55.121 --> 00:29:57.565
have we tested those modules
at the same speed

00:29:57.566 --> 00:30:00.330
or have we tested
another part of code?

00:30:00.331 --> 00:30:04.120
How do we know that the code
quality is so good on that?"

00:30:04.121 --> 00:30:06.730
So the input to the decision.

00:30:06.731 --> 00:30:09.710
At the beginning we had
this as a manual input,

00:30:09.711 --> 00:30:12.210
right now we build in more of

00:30:12.211 --> 00:30:15.865
a prediction models that
could tell that for instance,

00:30:15.866 --> 00:30:19.330
if we have exactly the same
heatmap but on a test cases,

00:30:19.331 --> 00:30:21.720
we could show that in
the same week we have

00:30:21.721 --> 00:30:24.930
usually made a
change in module A,

00:30:24.931 --> 00:30:26.980
and then usually test case

00:30:26.981 --> 00:30:30.080
X have reacted on that sort
of found the defect.

00:30:30.081 --> 00:30:33.430
We could prioritize test cases
based on culture and

00:30:33.431 --> 00:30:35.025
try to provide this
as a feedback to

00:30:35.026 --> 00:30:36.800
the test leader saying, "Look,

00:30:36.801 --> 00:30:40.170
this is how your test strategy
should be designed

00:30:40.171 --> 00:30:41.680
in the next few weeks

00:30:41.681 --> 00:30:44.700
if your changes are
going to be like that."

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.010
That kind of approach
let us also to

00:30:48.011 --> 00:30:50.440
look in at the standards
for the automotive.

00:30:50.441 --> 00:30:53.400
So, Draper who is working
for Volvo has been looking

00:30:53.401 --> 00:30:56.320
at the changes in
the AUTOSAR standard,

00:30:56.321 --> 00:30:59.565
which is a huge standard kind

00:30:59.566 --> 00:31:01.875
of like an operating
system for a car.

00:31:01.876 --> 00:31:03.940
You could see this that way.

00:31:03.941 --> 00:31:05.935
What he could do with

00:31:05.936 --> 00:31:10.945
his change waves and with
his optimization of the code,

00:31:10.946 --> 00:31:13.640
he could predict the impact of

00:31:13.641 --> 00:31:15.760
a particular change
in the standard on

00:31:15.761 --> 00:31:18.620
the cost of a
development of a car.

00:31:18.621 --> 00:31:22.795
So, that standard could
say that for instance,

00:31:22.796 --> 00:31:25.345
we add one attribute
to some construct or

00:31:25.346 --> 00:31:28.195
some function in this,
say operating system,

00:31:28.196 --> 00:31:30.615
and he could predict
how much that will

00:31:30.616 --> 00:31:34.615
cost the company in terms
of change management,

00:31:34.616 --> 00:31:36.345
in terms of new development,

00:31:36.346 --> 00:31:39.795
is the new version of
the operating system or

00:31:39.796 --> 00:31:41.115
standard going to be backward

00:31:41.116 --> 00:31:43.355
compatible with the
software we have,

00:31:43.356 --> 00:31:44.865
and so on and so on.

00:31:44.866 --> 00:31:46.740
But he could also do is
that he could look at

00:31:46.741 --> 00:31:49.900
the Pareto front and could

00:31:49.901 --> 00:31:53.210
identify the best
combination of features.

00:31:53.211 --> 00:31:55.520
So, if you develop
the car and say,

00:31:55.521 --> 00:31:57.355
I want to have
this particular feature,

00:31:57.356 --> 00:31:59.410
you could find that in
this and say, okay,

00:31:59.411 --> 00:32:00.845
if I want to have this feature,

00:32:00.846 --> 00:32:02.300
all of these features I

00:32:02.301 --> 00:32:03.990
get for free because
I still need to

00:32:03.991 --> 00:32:07.485
develop that because of
that particular feature, right?

00:32:07.486 --> 00:32:09.295
So, that gives you a discovery of

00:32:09.296 --> 00:32:11.510
new business possibilities when

00:32:11.511 --> 00:32:13.590
you develop those kind
of big systems, right?

00:32:13.591 --> 00:32:17.395
Then we talk about 100 to

00:32:17.396 --> 00:32:19.335
200 different computers and

00:32:19.336 --> 00:32:22.205
tens of thousands of
different functions in the car.

00:32:22.206 --> 00:32:24.510
So it gets pretty
complex with that.

00:32:24.511 --> 00:32:28.365
Thanks to this analysis
of the changes,

00:32:28.366 --> 00:32:30.660
he could basically quickly
predict and provide

00:32:30.661 --> 00:32:33.535
more information
to people on that.

00:32:33.536 --> 00:32:35.310
Based on that model
he could also say

00:32:35.311 --> 00:32:37.005
that if we want to implement,

00:32:37.006 --> 00:32:39.690
say this feature and what's

00:32:39.691 --> 00:32:41.220
the cost to implement
the next one

00:32:41.221 --> 00:32:42.985
and what is this difference,

00:32:42.986 --> 00:32:44.430
so what do we get if we move

00:32:44.431 --> 00:32:47.150
on to the next level so to say,

00:32:47.151 --> 00:32:53.000
and identified also the sort
of optimum when the cost

00:32:53.001 --> 00:32:55.835
is basically much more than
the benefit that you get

00:32:55.836 --> 00:32:59.655
from those features that you
shipped to the customers,

00:32:59.656 --> 00:33:04.905
which is pretty interesting
for car manufacturers.

00:33:04.906 --> 00:33:07.275
But on the other hand,
he looked at

00:33:07.276 --> 00:33:09.480
the very similar thing
but he looked

00:33:09.481 --> 00:33:12.190
at that from the perspective
of requirements, right?

00:33:12.191 --> 00:33:16.505
So, if you have 100 to 1,000
requirements specification,

00:33:16.506 --> 00:33:18.090
you would like to
quickly find out

00:33:18.091 --> 00:33:20.865
the requirements that
are connected that are

00:33:20.866 --> 00:33:23.085
incoherent that are really badly

00:33:23.086 --> 00:33:26.295
written to focus
all your effort on that.

00:33:26.296 --> 00:33:29.010
So, he developed that model

00:33:29.011 --> 00:33:32.889
based on simple measurements
of requirements,

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:36.719
kind of like a very basic
natural language processing,

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:38.489
but without any semantics,

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:39.980
and he could build that part of

00:33:39.981 --> 00:33:42.840
the toolchain at the company.

00:33:42.841 --> 00:33:43.920
So, right now when

00:33:43.921 --> 00:33:46.750
this company is working
with requirements,

00:33:46.751 --> 00:33:48.470
when they specify
the requirement,

00:33:48.471 --> 00:33:50.730
they get to directly
very quick feedback

00:33:50.731 --> 00:33:53.665
saying how good or how
bad that requirement is,

00:33:53.666 --> 00:33:56.940
and they also could get
an impact of why this is

00:33:56.941 --> 00:34:01.270
a good or a bad
requirement very quickly.

00:34:01.271 --> 00:34:04.980
I could give you an example
of that is for instance,

00:34:04.981 --> 00:34:06.710
if you are implementing

00:34:06.711 --> 00:34:08.370
something and then
you say that in

00:34:08.371 --> 00:34:13.280
your new track you should
implement standard 25026,

00:34:13.281 --> 00:34:16.145
which is a very nice requirement
if you write it,

00:34:16.146 --> 00:34:17.700
but then you open up the standard

00:34:17.701 --> 00:34:20.730
25026 that's like 2,000 pages.

00:34:20.731 --> 00:34:23.875
So, what do you mean by
implement the standard?

00:34:23.876 --> 00:34:26.185
In fact, which page?

00:34:26.186 --> 00:34:28.740
So, this model would catch
the situation and say,

00:34:28.741 --> 00:34:30.365
look, in this requirement,

00:34:30.366 --> 00:34:32.050
there's something
fishy, looks very

00:34:32.051 --> 00:34:35.375
nice but it's very
inspecific, right?

00:34:35.376 --> 00:34:37.540
You need to be very clear
about which page or

00:34:37.541 --> 00:34:39.505
which area of the standard

00:34:39.506 --> 00:34:41.425
you should implement
this so to say.

00:34:41.426 --> 00:34:44.590
Which saves the company's
hundreds of engineer in

00:34:44.591 --> 00:34:47.425
hours in backtracking
requirements that

00:34:47.426 --> 00:34:50.010
should be specified to be
better at the beginning

00:34:50.011 --> 00:34:54.295
of the development cycles.

00:34:54.296 --> 00:34:57.295
Something that
my colleague Miriam is

00:34:57.296 --> 00:35:01.660
working on mostly is
now looking at that.

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:05.700
If you look at
the professional companies

00:35:05.701 --> 00:35:08.075
that develop software like you,

00:35:08.076 --> 00:35:09.590
you have coding guidelines what

00:35:09.591 --> 00:35:12.040
people can do and
cannot do in the code,

00:35:12.041 --> 00:35:14.190
and you can use of
course style checkers

00:35:14.191 --> 00:35:16.440
to check what many
of those but many of

00:35:16.441 --> 00:35:19.385
the guidelines cannot
really be done using

00:35:19.386 --> 00:35:21.045
style checkers because writing

00:35:21.046 --> 00:35:23.120
the rule for that would
be very difficult,

00:35:23.121 --> 00:35:25.300
it's not straightforward
to do that.

00:35:25.301 --> 00:35:27.175
So, what my colleague is doing

00:35:27.176 --> 00:35:29.110
is he's building a
machine learning

00:35:29.111 --> 00:35:33.050
model to train how to
recognize violations

00:35:33.051 --> 00:35:37.270
in the design by providing
examples of a code.

00:35:37.271 --> 00:35:39.445
So when you do
a review and get it,

00:35:39.446 --> 00:35:42.375
you look at the code that's
been reviewed and say,

00:35:42.376 --> 00:35:44.405
this is a good code,
that is a bad code,

00:35:44.406 --> 00:35:46.335
and then this machine
learning algorithm

00:35:46.336 --> 00:35:48.030
learns which is
a good and bad code,

00:35:48.031 --> 00:35:50.715
so the next time you

00:35:50.716 --> 00:35:52.400
get the supporting Garrett
saying, "Look,

00:35:52.401 --> 00:35:54.530
this is a probably rule violation

00:35:54.531 --> 00:35:57.140
in that code or that
code looks good to me."

00:35:57.141 --> 00:36:00.465
So, over time that
review speed gets

00:36:00.466 --> 00:36:04.110
very much shorten because

00:36:04.111 --> 00:36:06.340
you don't have to read
the codes anymore,

00:36:06.341 --> 00:36:08.470
the machine learning
algorithm gives you

00:36:08.471 --> 00:36:12.310
a good example of what is
a violation or not on it.

00:36:12.311 --> 00:36:14.485
Of course since this
is all based on

00:36:14.486 --> 00:36:15.690
probabilities you never get

00:36:15.691 --> 00:36:17.385
100 percent correctness, right?

00:36:17.386 --> 00:36:20.190
So, if we will look at how

00:36:20.191 --> 00:36:23.690
far we can go for
very big code bases,

00:36:23.691 --> 00:36:26.215
we need to train an algorithm,

00:36:26.216 --> 00:36:28.880
we say roughly 300 lines
of code and then we get

00:36:28.881 --> 00:36:32.975
roughly 70 percent accuracy
in recognizing,

00:36:32.976 --> 00:36:35.360
which for a company
that does review

00:36:35.361 --> 00:36:37.580
on a daily basis there's a lot

00:36:37.581 --> 00:36:40.635
of speedup because they

00:36:40.636 --> 00:36:43.610
get two out of three lines
that they identified,

00:36:43.611 --> 00:36:44.780
this is actually
true positive and

00:36:44.781 --> 00:36:46.295
one line is false positive,

00:36:46.296 --> 00:36:48.910
but that's okay
because they still can

00:36:48.911 --> 00:36:52.465
take the stand on their own.

00:36:52.466 --> 00:36:56.075
The novelty of this is
that what we do is,

00:36:56.076 --> 00:36:58.965
we don't use any static models,

00:36:58.966 --> 00:37:01.280
is we would teach

00:37:01.281 --> 00:37:02.900
the machine learning
based on the codes.

00:37:02.901 --> 00:37:05.730
So we show the
algorithm the code,

00:37:05.731 --> 00:37:08.195
and we tag the lines which
are good and which are bad,

00:37:08.196 --> 00:37:10.775
and then it builds
a model by itself.

00:37:10.776 --> 00:37:13.625
Looking at things like,
which words are used,

00:37:13.626 --> 00:37:17.460
what kind of patterns
are used in the code,

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:20.030
how long the lines are,

00:37:20.031 --> 00:37:21.700
how many brackets when using

00:37:21.701 --> 00:37:24.010
a single line of code
and things like that.

00:37:24.011 --> 00:37:28.070
Yeah.

00:37:28.071 --> 00:37:29.295
And again thanks to

00:37:29.296 --> 00:37:30.370
the collaboration with

00:37:30.371 --> 00:37:31.940
different companies in
the software center,

00:37:31.941 --> 00:37:34.420
we could test
exactly the same rules at

00:37:34.421 --> 00:37:36.590
different companies and look at

00:37:36.591 --> 00:37:39.264
how they think about
coding violations.

00:37:39.265 --> 00:37:41.360
Because I know, if you
do code reviews, but,

00:37:41.361 --> 00:37:43.050
if you've done code review,

00:37:43.051 --> 00:37:46.015
you know the rules look
very simple on paper.

00:37:46.016 --> 00:37:48.430
But, then, if you look
at the code and have to

00:37:48.431 --> 00:37:51.060
decide if the rule is violated or

00:37:51.061 --> 00:37:55.280
not it's not always that
straightforward because

00:37:55.281 --> 00:37:57.400
sometimes there is
still quite a lot of

00:37:57.401 --> 00:38:00.435
interpretation how the
rules should be done.

00:38:00.436 --> 00:38:04.985
Thanks to using machine
learning and decision trees,

00:38:04.986 --> 00:38:07.930
one could actually
backtrack how things are

00:38:07.931 --> 00:38:11.990
working and when things
are going bad so to say.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:16.335
You don't have any questions

00:38:16.336 --> 00:38:20.490
or you've already
done that right?.

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:25.250
Okay. So, I'm preaching
to the choir.

00:38:25.251 --> 00:38:28.075
What we did also is, we started

00:38:28.076 --> 00:38:30.825
the patterns of how measurement

00:38:30.826 --> 00:38:33.375
information is actually
spread across the companies.

00:38:33.376 --> 00:38:37.380
So, instead of having
big monitors at landscapes

00:38:37.381 --> 00:38:38.905
that everyone looks at and no one

00:38:38.906 --> 00:38:41.455
really gets a lot of
information from,

00:38:41.456 --> 00:38:43.595
we started who is communicating

00:38:43.596 --> 00:38:45.710
with whom in a
company and then we

00:38:45.711 --> 00:38:49.860
could direct the information
directly to the people,

00:38:49.861 --> 00:38:51.700
who are interested on that.

00:38:51.701 --> 00:38:55.460
So looking at- [inaudible].

00:38:55.461 --> 00:38:56.865
&gt;&gt; Could be through
the communications,

00:38:56.866 --> 00:38:58.170
could be through the dashboards

00:38:58.171 --> 00:39:00.030
that automatically
adapt where people

00:39:00.031 --> 00:39:04.680
sit, different things, right?.

00:39:04.681 --> 00:39:07.180
Yeah. And then we

00:39:07.181 --> 00:39:10.500
also look at things when
people build dashboards.

00:39:10.501 --> 00:39:12.550
So, think about, for example,

00:39:12.551 --> 00:39:14.740
if they have to
decide between using

00:39:14.741 --> 00:39:18.510
the PowerBI or
a gadget or an email.

00:39:18.511 --> 00:39:20.000
How do they decide today?

00:39:20.001 --> 00:39:22.390
Well, today, they
basically look at what

00:39:22.391 --> 00:39:26.405
the purpose of that is and
find out what they want to do.

00:39:26.406 --> 00:39:29.655
But, we have this model

00:39:29.656 --> 00:39:31.815
that helps them to
choose quite quickly.

00:39:31.816 --> 00:39:34.235
So, it's like you can see
like a slider when you

00:39:34.236 --> 00:39:38.040
configure buying a new car or
buying a new mobile phone.

00:39:38.041 --> 00:39:40.475
We do exactly the same thing
for a dashboard.

00:39:40.476 --> 00:39:41.845
So, you do play around with

00:39:41.846 --> 00:39:44.140
different parameters and
then by the end of the day,

00:39:44.141 --> 00:39:47.950
you get an idea about
what kind of dashboard you are

00:39:47.951 --> 00:39:50.810
building and it could
also help you in

00:39:50.811 --> 00:39:52.450
some cases to choose what kind of

00:39:52.451 --> 00:39:55.260
measurements one could use.

00:39:55.261 --> 00:39:57.500
Based on selling those patterns,

00:39:57.501 --> 00:39:59.270
we also found out that

00:39:59.271 --> 00:40:00.720
instead of working with

00:40:00.721 --> 00:40:02.835
measurements in
terms of dashboards,

00:40:02.836 --> 00:40:04.950
quite many people
actually want to work

00:40:04.951 --> 00:40:07.010
with measurements in
terms of a cloud.

00:40:07.011 --> 00:40:08.080
So, they would like to have

00:40:08.081 --> 00:40:10.985
their own private
measurement system put

00:40:10.986 --> 00:40:13.990
in our own cloud environment,

00:40:13.991 --> 00:40:15.270
which is extremely simple,

00:40:15.271 --> 00:40:17.675
but still quite powerful and then

00:40:17.676 --> 00:40:20.120
spread the information
to other colleagues

00:40:20.121 --> 00:40:22.450
in a very simple way that will be

00:40:22.451 --> 00:40:26.285
automatically updated and
automatically spread out.

00:40:26.286 --> 00:40:30.480
We found out that it could
reduce up to 50% of the cost

00:40:30.481 --> 00:40:33.410
of measurement for the company

00:40:33.411 --> 00:40:35.175
and then increase, of course,

00:40:35.176 --> 00:40:36.315
empowerment of the teams,

00:40:36.316 --> 00:40:39.790
who want to have their own
measurements instead of having

00:40:39.791 --> 00:40:43.760
a central body [inaudible].

00:40:43.761 --> 00:40:46.365
That is based, of course,
on very simple technology.

00:40:46.366 --> 00:40:48.125
So, it's not like Azure cloud.

00:40:48.126 --> 00:40:49.425
It's much, much simpler.

00:40:49.426 --> 00:40:53.150
It's maybe 1,000 lines

00:40:53.151 --> 00:40:56.990
of code of the cloud environment
that we use. Sorry?

00:40:56.991 --> 00:40:57.585
&gt;&gt;  It's not a lot.

00:40:57.586 --> 00:41:00.000
&gt;&gt; No. I think that is not a lot.

00:41:00.001 --> 00:41:02.035
That says extremely
simple crowd, right?

00:41:02.036 --> 00:41:04.080
But, it's extremely powerful

00:41:04.081 --> 00:41:05.545
for that particular
purpose, right?

00:41:05.546 --> 00:41:06.760
Because what it basically does,

00:41:06.761 --> 00:41:09.280
it spreads around
different messages to

00:41:09.281 --> 00:41:10.665
different agents and then

00:41:10.666 --> 00:41:13.380
tells them to update
measurement systems, right?

00:41:13.381 --> 00:41:15.545
So, it's extremely simple,

00:41:15.546 --> 00:41:17.485
but extremely powerful and

00:41:17.486 --> 00:41:20.435
the things that we
have discovered,

00:41:20.436 --> 00:41:22.835
thanks to that kind
of environment,

00:41:22.836 --> 00:41:25.130
was that it's a much more

00:41:25.131 --> 00:41:28.440
robust to changes
in the environment.

00:41:28.441 --> 00:41:30.555
So, for example, if
one computer goes down,

00:41:30.556 --> 00:41:33.425
it's extremely easy to just
recover the measurements.

00:41:33.426 --> 00:41:34.590
In the previous architecture,

00:41:34.591 --> 00:41:36.970
which we had was much
more server-based,

00:41:36.971 --> 00:41:39.150
so, we had to have backups
and things like that.

00:41:39.151 --> 00:41:40.440
Now, all of that is, of course,

00:41:40.441 --> 00:41:43.440
automatically because you
have it in the cloud, so,

00:41:43.441 --> 00:41:46.420
you have a lot of the same kind
of computers that

00:41:46.421 --> 00:41:49.950
are using exactly the same kind
of measurements,

00:41:49.951 --> 00:41:52.150
so, you can easily get it back.

00:41:52.151 --> 00:41:56.390
So, to wrap up,

00:41:56.391 --> 00:41:59.315
yeah, that's what we do
together with my colleagues.

00:41:59.316 --> 00:42:02.400
This autonomous
measurement systems

00:42:02.401 --> 00:42:03.700
that change themselves.

00:42:03.701 --> 00:42:07.450
That change the way the
companies work with measurement

00:42:07.451 --> 00:42:10.000
instead of having dedicated

00:42:10.001 --> 00:42:12.130
large teams to
maintain measurements,

00:42:12.131 --> 00:42:16.320
we could achieve that with
very, very small teams.

00:42:16.321 --> 00:42:19.530
I'm not fan of size of the team,

00:42:19.531 --> 00:42:21.640
but the size of
the team, of course,

00:42:21.641 --> 00:42:23.120
determines the cost
of the measurement

00:42:23.121 --> 00:42:24.940
program and in every company,

00:42:24.941 --> 00:42:26.570
the measurement program
is not really something

00:42:26.571 --> 00:42:28.695
that you sell, right?

00:42:28.696 --> 00:42:30.780
I mean, Ericsson doesn't
sell measurement system.

00:42:30.781 --> 00:42:31.890
They sell Telecom notes.

00:42:31.891 --> 00:42:35.485
[inaudible] doesn't sell measurement
system, they sell cars, right?

00:42:35.486 --> 00:42:38.520
So, they would like to have
this same information with

00:42:38.521 --> 00:42:40.410
as little cost as possible and

00:42:40.411 --> 00:42:42.580
that's exactly what we're doing.

00:42:42.581 --> 00:42:46.950
So, maximizing the benefit
with the minimum cost on it.

00:42:46.951 --> 00:42:51.420
So, with that, thanks a lot.

00:42:51.421 --> 00:42:53.675
If you have any questions
or any feedback,

00:42:53.676 --> 00:42:56.460
I'll be super happy to hear that.

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:02.750
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:43:02.751 --> 00:43:03.975
&gt;&gt; companies A and B.

00:43:03.976 --> 00:43:05.200
You learn something from

00:43:05.201 --> 00:43:09.325
Company A and you want
to use it on company B.

00:43:09.326 --> 00:43:11.805
Is company A okay with you taking

00:43:11.806 --> 00:43:15.080
learnings and applying
it to company B?

00:43:15.081 --> 00:43:16.550
&gt;&gt; Most often yes and in

00:43:16.551 --> 00:43:19.160
some percentage that's
never a problem.

00:43:19.161 --> 00:43:21.320
&gt;&gt; So, there are, I mean,
part of the goal is

00:43:21.321 --> 00:43:23.655
also to build
a transferable model?

00:43:23.656 --> 00:43:25.220
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:32.130
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] improve
their way of measuring and

00:43:32.131 --> 00:43:35.345
somehow now the liability of
the production has gone up,

00:43:35.346 --> 00:43:37.835
why wouldn't they want
to let this information

00:43:37.836 --> 00:43:40.745
go and be useful for
the competitors?

00:43:40.746 --> 00:43:42.770
Because they can make better

00:43:42.771 --> 00:43:46.380
[inaudible] level cars,
that's their IP.

00:43:47.586 --> 00:43:51.425
&gt;&gt; In the software center,
the whole thing is built on

00:43:51.426 --> 00:43:54.045
the idea that there are

00:43:54.046 --> 00:43:56.500
no competitors inside and

00:43:56.501 --> 00:43:58.040
if you want to join
a software center,

00:43:58.041 --> 00:44:00.540
all the companies have
the right to veto.

00:44:00.541 --> 00:44:02.870
So, for example, if
you have a Volvo in

00:44:02.871 --> 00:44:04.450
software center and BMW would

00:44:04.451 --> 00:44:06.440
come over and say I want
to be part of that,

00:44:06.441 --> 00:44:08.345
Volvo could easily say sorry.

00:44:08.346 --> 00:44:11.600
Their competitors will veto
that. So they cannot join.

00:44:11.601 --> 00:44:13.230
So, all the companies that are in

00:44:13.231 --> 00:44:17.000
that collaboration are not
competitors. Meaning that-.

00:44:17.001 --> 00:44:18.465
&gt;&gt; They work in
different domains.

00:44:18.466 --> 00:44:19.830
&gt;&gt; They work in
different domains.

00:44:19.831 --> 00:44:21.250
&gt;&gt; So, from this example,

00:44:21.251 --> 00:44:23.480
A and B are not competitors.

00:44:23.481 --> 00:44:25.280
That is why they're
linked to same data?

00:44:25.281 --> 00:44:27.070
&gt;&gt; Exactly. And, of course,

00:44:27.071 --> 00:44:28.560
they don't share all of

00:44:28.561 --> 00:44:30.885
the data completely open,
for example, right?

00:44:30.886 --> 00:44:32.960
Ericsson doesn't
give source code to

00:44:32.961 --> 00:44:34.895
Volvo and vice versa, right?

00:44:34.896 --> 00:44:36.290
They could meet on a workshop

00:44:36.291 --> 00:44:37.570
and show each other their code,

00:44:37.571 --> 00:44:40.595
but they're not giving
that to them, right?

00:44:40.596 --> 00:44:44.420
&gt;&gt; How often do you see
that models need tweaking.

00:44:44.421 --> 00:44:47.675
Suppose, you build like
estimated defects model

00:44:47.676 --> 00:44:50.915
in Volvo and you want
to apply in Ericsson.

00:44:50.916 --> 00:44:53.400
Does it apply off
the box or is there

00:44:53.401 --> 00:44:55.730
a fair amount of
manual tuning that goes on

00:44:55.731 --> 00:44:58.355
[inaudible] methodology the same

00:44:58.356 --> 00:45:00.480
or it is the model
itself just the same.

00:45:00.481 --> 00:45:02.930
[inaudible] takes the same
coefficient [inaudible].

00:45:02.931 --> 00:45:06.000
&gt;&gt; Now. It's mostly the
methodology that is the same.

00:45:06.090 --> 00:45:10.185
All of the companies have,
even though they have,

00:45:10.186 --> 00:45:11.480
maybe in some cases,

00:45:11.481 --> 00:45:13.910
very similar set of tools,

00:45:13.911 --> 00:45:15.815
they do things differently.

00:45:15.816 --> 00:45:17.180
So, for example,

00:45:17.181 --> 00:45:19.110
just the sheer number
of test cases that they

00:45:19.111 --> 00:45:20.795
have could be so different

00:45:20.796 --> 00:45:23.630
that the coefficient
would not make sense.

00:45:23.631 --> 00:45:27.435
So, based on that, but
the methodology is the same.

00:45:27.436 --> 00:45:29.155
And, of course, if you look at

00:45:29.156 --> 00:45:32.730
how much effort is spent
on coming up to the model,

00:45:32.731 --> 00:45:35.710
of course, coming up with
a methodology, for instance,

00:45:35.711 --> 00:45:39.565
trying out different models
is what it takes time, right?

00:45:39.566 --> 00:45:40.905
And then if you know

00:45:40.906 --> 00:45:42.935
exactly what kind of
steps you can do,

00:45:42.936 --> 00:45:45.200
it's much easier to replicate.

00:45:45.201 --> 00:45:46.730
It takes maybe 10 percent of the

00:45:46.731 --> 00:45:48.370
time of the original time.

00:45:48.371 --> 00:45:53.525
&gt;&gt; So, every model
that is getting

00:45:53.526 --> 00:45:55.425
reclaimed every time
you want to transfer

00:45:55.426 --> 00:45:59.540
technology to a new
company, for example,

00:45:59.541 --> 00:46:01.505
Writing better code,

00:46:01.506 --> 00:46:03.040
the example you gave earlier,

00:46:03.041 --> 00:46:04.880
I can imagine writing code for

00:46:04.881 --> 00:46:07.320
telecommunication company
can be different

00:46:07.321 --> 00:46:12.060
from writing code for
an automobile company.

00:46:12.061 --> 00:46:12.520
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:46:12.521 --> 00:46:14.295
&gt;&gt; So then, you have to manually

00:46:14.296 --> 00:46:16.470
or ask the company to be fully

00:46:16.471 --> 00:46:20.985
engaged again in the beginning
and retrain the models.

00:46:20.986 --> 00:46:22.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's true.

00:46:22.861 --> 00:46:28.470
So, if you look at
this particular example of that,

00:46:28.471 --> 00:46:30.750
what we were so happy
about is that it

00:46:30.751 --> 00:46:33.150
takes only five trials,

00:46:33.151 --> 00:46:35.200
so to say, so five
learning cycles to

00:46:35.201 --> 00:46:37.760
get to a pretty decent
accuracy of the model.

00:46:37.761 --> 00:46:41.160
Because every company, and
even within the same company,

00:46:41.161 --> 00:46:43.485
different products have
different coding guidelines.

00:46:43.486 --> 00:46:45.040
Some products are written in C,

00:46:45.041 --> 00:46:47.100
some products are written in C++,

00:46:47.101 --> 00:46:49.130
they have completely
different rules.

00:46:49.131 --> 00:46:51.820
So, what we are focusing
on is to minimize this.

00:46:51.821 --> 00:46:55.580
So ideally, we would
have just one run.

00:46:55.581 --> 00:46:57.110
Show me the code and then

00:46:57.111 --> 00:46:58.910
I teach the model then I run it,

00:46:58.911 --> 00:47:02.000
but that's maybe a topic,
we cannot do that.

00:47:02.001 --> 00:47:04.280
So, up to five, we need
to have at least today,

00:47:04.281 --> 00:47:07.195
five routes of doing that.

00:47:07.196 --> 00:47:10.610
The whole idea that we're
working on is, as I mentioned,

00:47:10.611 --> 00:47:13.350
to have a sample learning
so that over time,

00:47:13.351 --> 00:47:15.330
the rules will change and

00:47:15.331 --> 00:47:18.705
then the model will
also change, so to say.

00:47:18.706 --> 00:47:21.050
So, right now, we're trying
to find out the break point

00:47:21.051 --> 00:47:24.575
when the model should actually
automatically adjust,

00:47:24.576 --> 00:47:26.740
when is the model not
good enough anymore,

00:47:26.741 --> 00:47:29.130
so that the model
could think about

00:47:29.131 --> 00:47:32.250
it or come up with
that idea by itself.

00:47:32.251 --> 00:47:35.330
But the training is really

00:47:35.331 --> 00:47:37.915
important because the rules
are extremely different,

00:47:37.916 --> 00:47:40.745
and they call, how they apply
and how they're understood,

00:47:40.746 --> 00:47:42.365
they also very different.

00:47:42.366 --> 00:47:44.475
&gt;&gt; The metrics
should be different.

00:47:44.476 --> 00:47:45.930
&gt;&gt; No, the metrics inside here,

00:47:45.931 --> 00:47:47.760
they're identical for
all the companies.

00:47:47.761 --> 00:47:51.760
The set of features that
we use in the model,

00:47:51.761 --> 00:47:54.325
they're exactly the same.

00:47:54.326 --> 00:47:56.670
But how you write
the code, for example,

00:47:56.671 --> 00:47:59.795
how do you use capital letters

00:47:59.796 --> 00:48:03.560
or underscores will differ
from company to company,

00:48:03.561 --> 00:48:04.855
from product to product,

00:48:04.856 --> 00:48:07.355
and the data types
that use also differ.

00:48:07.356 --> 00:48:08.980
So, instead of coding

00:48:08.981 --> 00:48:12.065
that heart into
the machine learning,

00:48:12.066 --> 00:48:14.850
we've tried to extract that,

00:48:14.851 --> 00:48:17.560
so we use things
like bag of words

00:48:17.561 --> 00:48:21.240
to understand which language
you basically use.

00:48:21.241 --> 00:48:23.110
What is a variable?
What is the type?

00:48:23.111 --> 00:48:25.470
What is the name?
What is a function?

00:48:25.471 --> 00:48:28.290
If you just analyze
the code, of course,

00:48:28.291 --> 00:48:29.780
the keywords will
pop up much more

00:48:29.781 --> 00:48:31.730
frequently than any other words,

00:48:31.731 --> 00:48:33.265
because keywords are the same,

00:48:33.266 --> 00:48:34.990
but names of variables change,

00:48:34.991 --> 00:48:38.490
so we learn using
those techniques.

00:48:38.491 --> 00:48:41.495
&gt;&gt; You mean you dynamically
identify the language?

00:48:41.496 --> 00:48:41.810
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:48:41.811 --> 00:48:44.280
&gt;&gt; Then apply the rules
for specific [inaudible].

00:48:44.281 --> 00:48:45.375
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:48:45.376 --> 00:48:47.105
&gt;&gt; In some cases, you can even,

00:48:47.106 --> 00:48:49.480
depends of course on
the rules, but in some cases,

00:48:49.481 --> 00:48:53.150
you use rules that you
trained on Java code on

00:48:53.151 --> 00:48:55.270
the C++ code because they

00:48:55.271 --> 00:48:58.345
are not that much
different in some cases.

00:48:58.346 --> 00:49:01.180
&gt;&gt; These checks for code,

00:49:01.181 --> 00:49:03.480
they are only trying
to find, let's say,

00:49:03.481 --> 00:49:06.950
unintended bad code
or bad mistakes,

00:49:06.951 --> 00:49:08.735
somebody changed the variable.

00:49:08.736 --> 00:49:11.840
How will you go about
trying to detect,

00:49:11.841 --> 00:49:15.250
let's say, malicious
fault in the code?

00:49:15.251 --> 00:49:18.620
Let's say in the case of
a model, like in diesel cars,

00:49:18.621 --> 00:49:21.110
they will now updating
the variables so that it

00:49:21.111 --> 00:49:23.715
reports low emission
that actually does,

00:49:23.716 --> 00:49:25.475
and this was maliciously put in.

00:49:25.476 --> 00:49:28.310
So, the software was
looking for code,

00:49:28.311 --> 00:49:30.035
quality isn't going
to catch that.

00:49:30.036 --> 00:49:33.020
How will you catch
that kind of problem?

00:49:33.021 --> 00:49:34.370
&gt;&gt; That I don't really know,

00:49:34.371 --> 00:49:36.500
because we didn't work with that.

00:49:36.501 --> 00:49:39.160
But I guess if you have
examples of a code that

00:49:39.161 --> 00:49:43.115
does this thing that could
be applicable as well.

00:49:43.116 --> 00:49:45.420
But we have not done with those.

00:49:45.760 --> 00:49:47.960
&gt;&gt; Because if you have seen that

00:49:47.961 --> 00:49:49.895
only the values for
these variables

00:49:49.896 --> 00:49:53.710
always are in the range of 0-50,

00:49:53.711 --> 00:49:55.640
and suddenly there's
a new update to

00:49:55.641 --> 00:49:58.430
the code and it makes
its value 5,000,

00:49:58.431 --> 00:50:00.785
will your system
flag that right now?

00:50:00.786 --> 00:50:03.030
&gt;&gt; Right now, no.

00:50:03.490 --> 00:50:06.575
If that's not the part
of the coding guideline,

00:50:06.576 --> 00:50:07.820
then it will never flag that.

00:50:07.821 --> 00:50:09.510
&gt;&gt; I see. Okay.

00:50:09.940 --> 00:50:13.040
&gt;&gt; How many people
worked on this project?

00:50:13.041 --> 00:50:17.670
&gt;&gt; On this project, it's
only two people working.

00:50:17.671 --> 00:50:20.775
&gt;&gt; I mean the whole
measurement system?

00:50:20.776 --> 00:50:23.480
&gt;&gt; We are, I think, 15,

00:50:23.481 --> 00:50:24.900
20 people in the team,

00:50:24.901 --> 00:50:27.790
both in academia and industry.

00:50:27.791 --> 00:50:30.250
In academia, we focused
mostly on the development of

00:50:30.251 --> 00:50:34.335
the meat of that,

00:50:34.336 --> 00:50:37.470
so the algorithms and our
colleagues from industry,

00:50:37.471 --> 00:50:39.160
they usually work
on applying that

00:50:39.161 --> 00:50:41.160
so they will take that
and apply that and

00:50:41.161 --> 00:50:43.650
see if that works for
them or not to give us

00:50:43.651 --> 00:50:46.755
feedback and help us
train the models.

00:50:46.756 --> 00:50:49.935
&gt;&gt; You requiring
the [inaudible] set, the models,

00:50:49.936 --> 00:50:52.940
and then you just release it and

00:50:52.941 --> 00:50:56.225
the development team can
collect all the data?

00:50:56.226 --> 00:50:56.570
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:50:56.571 --> 00:51:03.080
&gt;&gt; So, in the previous
part of the talk,

00:51:03.081 --> 00:51:06.199
you talked about some vastness
of the measurements,

00:51:06.200 --> 00:51:08.990
trying to figure out
which measurement should be

00:51:08.991 --> 00:51:14.165
more indicative or flagged.

00:51:14.166 --> 00:51:17.150
What models do you use for that?

00:51:17.151 --> 00:51:19.565
Because I can't
imagine those being

00:51:19.566 --> 00:51:22.400
thousands of measurements
that might be correlated,

00:51:22.401 --> 00:51:26.695
not correlated, what kind
of models do you use

00:51:26.696 --> 00:51:30.260
to figure out these connections

00:51:30.261 --> 00:51:32.080
between them as you
would they are in?

00:51:32.081 --> 00:51:34.805
&gt;&gt; We use extremely
simple models.

00:51:34.806 --> 00:51:36.530
So, we start with a PCA,

00:51:36.531 --> 00:51:39.930
and look which variables
are colinearly correlated,

00:51:39.931 --> 00:51:41.790
and then we provide
that feedback to

00:51:41.791 --> 00:51:44.660
the stakeholder saying,
"Look, those variables,

00:51:44.661 --> 00:51:47.835
they're always in the same
component," for example,

00:51:47.836 --> 00:51:49.490
and then they would say,

00:51:49.491 --> 00:51:50.860
"Yeah, but this metric,

00:51:50.861 --> 00:51:52.780
for example, should only

00:51:52.781 --> 00:51:54.730
be identified when it's
not correlated with

00:51:54.731 --> 00:51:57.100
something else," so
we don't want to see

00:51:57.101 --> 00:51:59.505
both of them in the
measurement system.

00:51:59.506 --> 00:52:00.620
We want to see, for example,

00:52:00.621 --> 00:52:03.420
the delta or deviation
between one or the other,

00:52:03.421 --> 00:52:05.470
or only the weeks when
they deviate somehow.

00:52:05.471 --> 00:52:08.255
But the statistical methods

00:52:08.256 --> 00:52:10.820
are not that complicated to use.

00:52:10.821 --> 00:52:12.980
&gt;&gt; Do you do
a separate analysis of

00:52:12.981 --> 00:52:15.770
which metrics or measurement,

00:52:15.771 --> 00:52:18.080
which measurements
affect which metrics?

00:52:18.081 --> 00:52:20.720
For example, if I

00:52:20.721 --> 00:52:22.115
see some measurements that are

00:52:22.116 --> 00:52:23.510
dependent for these metrics,

00:52:23.511 --> 00:52:26.825
they might not be dependent
on another different metric,

00:52:26.826 --> 00:52:28.850
do you join in that system?

00:52:28.851 --> 00:52:34.580
&gt;&gt; Not in that way, no.

00:52:34.581 --> 00:52:37.950
Well, we tried that but it
didn't really work out,

00:52:37.951 --> 00:52:40.410
because the models were
basically too complex.

00:52:40.411 --> 00:52:42.825
So, we used the graph theory

00:52:42.826 --> 00:52:46.600
to draw a graph of which metrics
are dependent on what,

00:52:46.601 --> 00:52:49.100
but the models were
too complex and we

00:52:49.101 --> 00:52:52.620
couldn't really get
any meaningful results.

00:52:53.560 --> 00:52:56.700
So, that's why we
use much simpler PCA

00:52:56.701 --> 00:53:01.370
and just statistics and then
flag things. [inaudible]

00:53:01.371 --> 00:53:02.890
&gt;&gt; Because your model
is only as good

00:53:02.891 --> 00:53:04.840
as your interpretation of it,

00:53:04.841 --> 00:53:07.190
so if you cannot interpret
those [inaudible] graphs,

00:53:07.191 --> 00:53:09.210
then you have to rely
on the statistics.

00:53:09.211 --> 00:53:10.590
&gt;&gt; Exactly. That's why we,

00:53:10.591 --> 00:53:12.035
for example, also in this,

00:53:12.036 --> 00:53:14.765
don't really go to deep learning
or neural networks,

00:53:14.766 --> 00:53:18.420
but we prefer models
like decision trees or,

00:53:18.421 --> 00:53:22.480
basically, decision
trees which can provide

00:53:22.481 --> 00:53:25.180
their own interpretation
why they classify

00:53:25.181 --> 00:53:28.140
a specific line as
a deviation and why not,

00:53:28.141 --> 00:53:31.920
so that we could use this
either as a input to training,

00:53:31.921 --> 00:53:33.530
if it's a false positive,

00:53:33.531 --> 00:53:35.480
or as understanding that "Okay,

00:53:35.481 --> 00:53:40.180
we are missing
a feature in our model

00:53:40.181 --> 00:53:43.120
so we need to add
something so that

00:53:43.121 --> 00:53:46.950
this flag does not get
recognized as a false positive."

00:53:46.951 --> 00:53:50.140
With neural network,
that's really difficult.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:52.210
The thing is also that for

00:53:52.211 --> 00:53:54.585
neural networks and on
a more complex model,

00:53:54.586 --> 00:53:57.450
you need to have
really huge datasets.

00:53:57.451 --> 00:54:00.560
You cannot really expect
an architect to sit

00:54:00.561 --> 00:54:03.420
down and talk 7 million
lines of code,

00:54:03.421 --> 00:54:06.515
which lines had
violations or not.

00:54:06.516 --> 00:54:10.190
So, 300 and 600 lines of code is

00:54:10.191 --> 00:54:11.585
probably max what you can ask

00:54:11.586 --> 00:54:13.640
someone to help you with tagging.

00:54:13.641 --> 00:54:16.370
So, we need to have a model
that you can trace back

00:54:16.371 --> 00:54:19.430
and to understand that if you,

00:54:19.431 --> 00:54:21.870
for example, the model
gives you a false positive,

00:54:21.871 --> 00:54:23.130
why is this a false positive.

00:54:23.131 --> 00:54:24.930
How should I provide
a counter example

00:54:24.931 --> 00:54:27.260
to teach the model
in a better way?

00:54:27.261 --> 00:54:29.570
You cannot do that
with a black box,

00:54:29.571 --> 00:54:31.670
with models that are black box.

00:54:31.671 --> 00:54:37.750
Hope that helps.

00:54:37.751 --> 00:54:38.860
&gt;&gt; If there are
no other questions,

00:54:38.861 --> 00:54:40.120
let's thank Professor
Miroslaw Staron.

00:54:40.121 --> 00:54:43.070
&gt;&gt; Okay. Thank you
very much.Thanks.

